Arcade bar: Flycast's libretro shell for our frontend (web/emulator/libretro.js), not EmulatorJS.

Applied after retrom-project/flycast-wasm's patches (see build.sh), whose wasm-jit-phase1 patch
made retro_run an EmulatorJS player: a wall-clock pacer that ran 0, 1 or 2 emulated frames per call
(owed frames carried with a fractional part, an emulation speed trimmed by +-0.5% to keep its
AudioWorklet queue filled), a HUD written into the page's DOM and its window._flyLog, and the
sound pushed from inside the core to an AudioWorklet of its own (shell/libretro/audiostream.cpp,
EM_JS fly_worklet_push, which needs `window`). The bar's worker paces frames itself, runs exactly
one per retro_run, replays frames for rollback and plays the sound it is given, so:

- retro_run: the pacer and the HUD are gone; it emulates once (core/emulator.cpp then stops the
  SH4 at the next vblank: patches/0002) and reports the frame (or a duplicate) as upstream does.
- audiostream.cpp: no AudioWorklet; the samples go to audio_batch_cb, as upstream.
- retro_get_system_av_info: the rates the machine really runs at. The frontend reads them once,
  at load, and paces every frame and resamples all the sound by them: a NAOMI's 31 kHz video
  makes a vblank every 3344830 SH4 cycles (59.794 Hz, not the NTSC 59.94 reported), and the AICA a
  sample every 4535 cycles (44101.4 Hz).
- States, saved and loaded between two retro_runs on the thread that runs the machine: without
  emu.stop()/emu.start() around them (stopping wrote the NVRAM files and fired the pause/resume
  events at every save), and with one size for the whole game (a state's size moves with the
  PowerVR's TA contexts, which made rollback slots allocated once too small): the first size plus
  room for a whole TA context (8 MB), the unused rest zeroed so a machine always makes the same
  bytes.
- Hooks for flycast/shim/vab_flycast.cpp, which does what RetroArch would for a core that draws
  with OpenGL and our frontend can't (it has no RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_HW_RENDER): the environment
  callback and the video callback are wrapped by the shim (vab_wrap_environment answers
  SET_HW_RENDER and the core options; vab_wrap_video reads frames back from WebGL), once the
  game is loaded vab_game_loaded() makes the context and calls the core's context_reset, and at
  the start of each retro_run vab_frame_start() collects a frame read back earlier, before this
  frame's GL commands are queued.

